  "cause": "**The market found the price of a constituency it had not been pricing, and it found it on Thursday.** The **BBC** reports that **around one trillion dollars** was wiped off the combined stock value of **seven of the world's biggest tech companies, including Apple and Tesla**, as tech stocks fell across Asia and Europe; the tech-heavy **Nasdaq** was down about **5.5%** on Thursday from the previous day's close. The BBC's correspondents describe the falls as concentrated in \"data centre buildout-adjacent stocks and Elon Musk-adjacent stocks\", wider in Asia and shallower in Europe, with **S&P 500 futures up** from that level and the index still about **4.6% higher than at the start of the year**. The framing in the BBC's own live coverage is blunt: \"AI's freedom era is over.\" **The vote that moved the market was local, and it was about generators.** **BBC Verify** reports that a US midterms voter poll found data centres a highly motivating issue, though well behind government corruption (90%) and the cost of living (89%) among \"crucial or major\" factors. The material case is specific: **Angelita Soriano's** Indiana home will sit across the street from a new **Amazon** data centre of **26 buildings** covering **more than a square mile**, with **782 diesel-powered generators** — *\"I don't want to be the guinea pig for this\"* — while **Maureen and Richard Bachtel** moved to an Arizona community to be closer to family and found construction starting nearby. Three states central to the AI build-out — **Texas, Georgia and Ohio** — elected Democrats as senators and governors who called for tighter regulation of data centres. **The analysts are already pricing the permitting regime rather than the technology.** **Cori Crider** of the **Future of Tech Institute** says the industry should expect new state leaders to put conditions on or \"even stop runaway data centre build\"; **Seema Shah** of **Principal Asset Management** says requirements to fund grid upgrades, meet tougher water standards or forgo tax incentives could \"raise development costs and slow new supply\"; **Heather West** of **Eurasia Group** expects permitted and under-construction projects to proceed but the \"next wave\" to face \"local fights, slower permitting, and pressure to make data centres pay for their own power\"; **Oxford Economics** notes the countervailing pull of tax revenue and construction jobs; **Deepak Surana** of **Futurum Group** expects \"a negotiation, not a moratorium\" in Texas. The BBC's correspondent adds the fact the market keeps passing over: the firms \"are burning through eye watering amounts of money on this infrastructure and not making anywhere near as much as they have to spend.\"",
