   "**A trillion dollars is the market discovering that the constraint on AI was never chips, it was a zoning board** — seven companies repriced in a day because three states changed hands; the direct effect is that the cost of capital for compute now carries a political beta no model had in it; the compound consequence is that every future capacity forecast has to be routed through county commissions, water districts and utility regulators — bodies that issue no guidance, take no analyst calls, and answer on a calendar measured in hearings",
   "**The backlash is not anti-technology, it is anti-externality, and the industry has been answering it with the wrong argument** — 782 diesel generators beside a house, a square mile of buildings, grid and water capacity paid for by everyone; the direct effect is that the objection is met by pointing at the technology's benefits, which nobody in the affected neighbourhood disputed; the compound consequence is that the political settlement will be written by whoever offers to pay for the grid — so the industry's cheapest available move, paying its own infrastructure costs up front and publicly, is the one its cost structure is least able to make after a year of record capex",
