  "verse": "And if strangers come to you from other peoples and search for protection with you, then take them in, provide for (feed and clothe) them and offer them safety and activity (work) if they can show (prove) that they are persecuted and their body and life have been threatened; also, do not leave those behind (leave helpless) who are able to show (prove) that they need your help.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 7, Verse 84",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the border and not Rwanda; it is the decision to convert a question of protection into a question of routing — to answer \"does this person need help\" with \"where can this person be sent\". §7:84 sets the test in the order that matters: protection first, provision second, work third, with the burden of proof on the person — a burden the verse accepts and the new regulation removes entirely by allowing transfer with no personal connection at all. The reversal is procedural: a named monitoring body with a remedy attached, a statutory maximum period with judicial review, a published count of transfers and returns, and a cost-per-removal figure printed beside the budget line. None of that closes a hub; all of it makes the hub answerable to the verse's actual question."
