  "cause": "**On his way out of office, a governor emptied the largest death row in the United States with a pen.** **California Governor Gavin Newsom** commuted the death sentences of **all 548 people on the state's death row**, converting them to **life in prison without the possibility of parole**. *\"We can now let these offenders die in obscurity while we forever remember and honour the victims,\"* he wrote. California has not executed anyone since **2006** and has executed **13** people since voters reinstated the death penalty in **1978**; the state has historically held the largest death-row population in the country. Newsom, who is **term limited** and in his final months, is projected to be succeeded by fellow Democrat **Xavier Becerra** after Tuesday's midterms, and has been discussed as a possible **2028** presidential contender. **The justification is not procedural, and it is unusually direct about cost and conscience.** Newsom wrote that he was exercising constitutional clemency authority; that \"after nearly 30 years in public office, I have come to understand the limits of what government can justly accomplish\"; that \"state-sanctioned killing is incompatible with California's values, undermines the foundations of our legal system and diminishes each of us\"; and that the state \"cannot morally kill them\". He called the system a failure \"by every measure\", said it \"has not and cannot be fairly applied, resulting in the conviction of innocent people\", and named the successor punishment as the most severe he can lawfully impose and the state can ethically enforce. He acknowledged the decision \"will anger and hurt some Californians\" and would \"always weigh heavily on me\": *\"As governor, I alone bear responsibility for this decision and its impact, just as I alone would bear responsibility for each execution.\"* Experts told the **BBC** the move is largely symbolic in a state that has not executed in two decades, though it could save millions of dollars in court costs each year. **Two facts sit beside each other here, and they are clemency and the calendar.** The commutations are final in a way almost nothing else in American law is: a governor's clemency cannot be undone by a successor, and 548 individual decisions were taken in a single act by a man who will never face the voters again. That is the instrument working exactly as designed — and it is also the same concentration of unreviewable power that, used in the other direction, is the thing the 2019 moratorium was meant to prevent.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A commutation converts a sentence into an outcome nobody can revisit** — 548 decisions, final, no appeal, no successor's signature required; the direct effect is that the state's most severe legal act has been performed once, wholesale, by one person; the compound consequence is that the precedent is symmetrical — the pen that emptied the row can be used by the next governor to fill it, and the only thing between the two is the character of the person elected, which is precisely the guarantee the death penalty's own supporters say is insufficient for a jury",
   "**The victims' families receive the state's decision as a fact and not as a process** — the letter concedes it \"will anger and hurt some Californians\"; the direct effect is that closure, which the sentence had promised as a date, becomes a status quo with no date at all; the compound consequence is that those families are now the constituency for restoration, and their grievance is legitimate and unanswerable by the argument that the system was a failure — because the failure they were told about is not the one they experienced",
   "**\"Largely symbolic\" is true about executions and false about money and law** — no execution since 2006, but capital litigation runs for decades; the direct effect is that 548 cases move from a death-penalty docket to a life-without-parole docket; the compound consequence is that the savings — millions a year in court costs, per the experts quoted — are the first real number attached to abolition in California, and a number that size invites the next fight to be about the budget rather than the principle, which is a fight abolition can win and a fight about mercy cannot",
   "**The precedent travels as a technique, not as a policy** — no legislature, no referendum, no campaign; a clemency power exercised at the end of a term; the direct effect is that other governors now have a demonstrated, legally clean method for the same act; the compound consequence is that abolition in the United States may proceed by executive pen rather than by vote — faster, reversible in one direction only, and permanently exposed to the charge that no electorate approved it, which is the charge that has undone other pen-and-phone settlements",
   "**A system declared a failure \"by every measure\" is not fixed by removing its end point** — innocence claims, racial disparity, cost, delay: all named; the direct effect is that the errors the governor described continue to be made at the front of the process, in charging and plea bargaining, where the death penalty was only the last stage; the compound consequence is that the reform removes the visible symptom and leaves the mechanism that produced it — a prosecutor's discretion no clemency power touches, and a plea system the same letter calls incapable of fair application"
  ],
  "verse": "No person of your kind (human being) is entitled to kill another person of your kind (human being) except if it is done in extreme defence… truly, no person of your kind (human being) is entitled to harm another person of your kind (human being), to commit cruelty (torture) on him or her or to kill him or her; whoever nevertheless deliberately (intentionally) kills (murders), their reward shall be the fire of destruction and misery which burns unceasingly in the admonishment (conscience).",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 4, Verse 122",
  "hinge": "§4:122 does two things the American argument usually separates: it forbids killing without an exception for the state, and it prescribes a thirty-year term with a review of whether the offender has changed, followed by assistance to the bereaved family. The verse is therefore neither the abolitionist's text nor the retentionist's — it rejects both the death chamber and a life sentence with no review, which is the sentence now imposed on 548 people. The change available is the one the letter does not contain: a review mechanism with a date, a route to assistance for families that does not depend on a death date, and a charging standard that reaches the prosecutorial discretion the governor named as the failure and left untouched. Clemency that stops at the prison door changes who is killed. It does not yet change how the state decides whom to pursue."
